theta_l-,theta_r+ = y- (x forces cancel)
theta_l+,theta_r- = y+ (x forces cancel)
theta_l-,theta_r- = x- (y forces cancel)
theta_l+,theta_r+ = x+ (y forces cancel)

x(theta_l,theta_r) = 0.5r_m(theta_l + theta_r)  (1mm of left displacement and 1mm of right displacement should be 1mm of nozzle displacement)
y(theta_l,theta_r) = 0.5r_m(theta_l - theta_r)

x'(theta_l,theta_r) = 0.5r_m(theta_l' + theta_r') 
y'(theta_l,theta_r) = 0.5r_m(theta_l' - theta_r')

We choose a state vector:
x_ = [theta_l', theta_r', theta_l, theta_r, i_ml, i_mr, x, y]^T
x_' = [theta_l'', theta_r'', theta_l', theta_r', i_ml', i_mr', x', y']^T

theta_l''(u_ml) = tau_l / J_m
with tau_l = K_m i_ml
with i_ml' = (1/L_m)(u_ml-R_m i_ml), i_ml(0)=0

-> f(x_):
theta_l'' = K_m i_ml / J_m
theta_r'' = K_m i_mr / J_m
theta_l' = theta_l'
theta_r' = theta_r'
i_ml' = (1/L_m)(u_ml-R_m i_ml)
i_mr' = (1/L_m)(u_mr-R_m i_mr)
x' = 0.5r_m(theta_l' + theta_r') 
y' = 0.5r_m(theta_l' - theta_r')

The input u is the motor voltages: u = [u_ml, u_mr]^T, B would be an 8x2 matrix (A 8x8)

So far we have not yet modeled the inertia of the toolhead, which has to be added to the model.
This can be modelled as an extra angular inertia added to the motors (distributed a constant 50/50, proof to be added)

The total added angular inertia is m r_m^2. Therefore the new equations are:
theta_l'' = K_m i_ml / (J_m + 0.5m r_m^2)
theta_r'' = K_m i_mr / (J_m + 0.5m r_m^2)
theta_l' = theta_l'
theta_r' = theta_r'
i_ml' = (1/L_m)(u_ml-R_m i_ml)
i_mr' = (1/L_m)(u_mr-R_m i_mr)
x' = 0.5r_m(theta_l' + theta_r') 
y' = 0.5r_m(theta_l' - theta_r')

The inertia would in practice add current back into the motor LR circuits, but this is assumed to be negligable.

A and B matrices can be easily derived from these equations, given x_' = Ax_+Bu

A = [[0,        0,      0,      0, K_m/(J_m + 0.5m r_m^2),      0,      0,      0],
     [0,        0,      0,      0,      0, K_m/(J_m + 0.5m r_m^2),      0,      0],
